okay big news from last night President  Obama experienced the first veto  override of his presidency nearly eight  years into his presidency so that is an  accomplishment in and of itself  President Obama previously vetoed  legislation which would allow relatives  of nine eleven victims to sue Saudi  Arabia when the president vetoes a bill  the House and Senate can then come  together and vote to override that veto  and that is exactly what happened last  night the House of Representatives voted  348 277 against President Obama's veto  and uh also the Senate voted 97 21 to  reject that veto meaning that the  justice against sponsors of terrorism  act will indeed become the law what this  law will do is grant an exception  there's this legal principle called  sovereign immunity in cases of terrorism  on us soul soil meaning if people from  foreign country X come to the US and  commit an act of terror Americans are  not able to sue country X because the  people who did the act of terror came  from their country this law will create  an exception to that legal principle  Saudi Arabia has for a long time denied  the allegations that hijackers who  attacked the US on 911 were backed by  the Saudi government a royal family uh  we know that 15 of the 19 hijackers were  Saudi nationals so that is going to  change now you might say well there's  probably some other side to this right i  mean it sounds good if we have people in  saudi arabia that we believe were funded  by the saudi government we want to be  able to sue them what is being said as  the counter is that this really does  open up the door to the u.s. being sued  when americans go to other countries and  perpetrate acts of terror or criminality  abroad and that sort of like the main  opposition that we've been hearing to  this uh that that is sort of where  the the debate is mostly CIA director  John Brennan said that there are quote  grave implications for national security  because if this were to also be applied  to the US all of a sudden you have a  potential liability sort of hanging out  there that the United States could be  sued in similar ways as to the way in  which Saudi Arabia could potentially be  sued as a result of this veto override  within hours of the vote nearly 30  senators signed a letter expressing that  they have some reservations about the  potential consequences of the law  including that the u.s. could be sued in  foreign courts what's fascinating to me  about this when I step back number one  it's hard for me to imagine feeling  really strongly about uh taking Saudi  Arabia out of harm's way as part of our  vile and totally depraved alliance with  Saudi Arabia but I had another thought  which is when we think about the  trans-pacific partnership right one of  the things that TPP will do is it will  make it so that transnational  corporations rather than being sued or  litigated within one of the countries in  which they operate instead will be  brought to some kind of international  arbitration and this is very very  dangerous as progressives we should be  very concerned about that because it  allows these countries and major  corporations through the influence that  they have to set up a system where it's  going to be really really favorable to  them much more favorable to be favorable  to them than the justice system of any  particular country in which they operate  it is getting us our circumventing the  individual justice systems of individual  countries this has a similar issue like  we are talking about when you say well  now American citizens can sue the  country of Saudi Arabia without being  citizens of that country without being  legal residents of that country you were  also creating a sort of extrajudicial at  least when it comes to the country level  mechanism and that is something that we  need to think about big picture now I  know that it's different in TPP and in  what we're talking about here with suing  Saudi  we're talking about two different  mechanisms but big picture that's a lot  of what is uh at issue here which is  going above and beyond the rules of the  country in which one is a citizen or a  permanent resident and what the  potential implications of that will be  so this this is going to take some time  to shake out uh those are some of the  concerns that we've heard so far Warby  Parker is offering a free five day home  try-on to give you the opportunity to  check out their glasses i personally  wear Warby Parker glasses when I'm not  wearing my contact lenses and I also  started with one of these free at home  try ons of five pairs of glasses you can  get your free home try-on and support  the david pakman show at Warby Parker  trial dot-com / TDPS I don't really like  going into glasses stores and trying on  glasses while the sales person sort of  hovers over me and I like more than 30  seconds to actually figure out whether I  like the glasses so with the Warby  Parker home try-on I was able to pick  five pairs that i liked put them on in  the comfort of my own home pick the one  I like the most and then Warby Parker  put my prescription lenses in there for  me a lot of people don't know that the  eyeglass industry is controlled by just  a few big corporations that keep prices  very high like three hundred dollars for  prescription glasses high which is just  insane Warby Parker glasses start at $95  that's what mine cost and for every pair  of glasses sold Warby Parker distributes  a pair of glasses to someone in need you  can try five pairs and see what you like  check out the at-home trial and support  our show by going to Warby Parker  dot-com / tdps it costs nothing
 

